<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Sorry, I Can't Help You. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sorry, I Can't Help You.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify written instructions for peers. Communication: Relay simplified explanations of lessons to absent peers.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify written instructions for peers using basic words and gestures (e.g., points to a pencil and says, "Ask for a pencil."). Can relay simple, polite request phrases to absent peers by acting them out when they return (e.g., mimes "Can I have this, please?"). Can rephrase polite request phrases using simple words (e.g., "Can I have this?" becomes "Please, pencil.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Mime and Guess the Request" game. The teacher mimes a polite request (e.g., needing a pencil, a piece of paper, help with a bag) and students try to guess the phrase, "Can I have a pencil, please?" or "Can you help me, please?". Students then mime expressing inability politely, "Sorry, I can't help you." activating prior knowledge of the theme "Sorry, I Can't Help You." and scenario "A Polite Request".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models polite requests and responses using clear gestures and simple language. For example, holding up one pencil and shaking head for "Sorry, I can't help you, I only have one pencil." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher also models blending for "kind" /k-aɪ-n-d/ when describing a "kind friend."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a student asking "Give me the pencil!" and another asking "Can I have a pencil, please?". CCQ: "Which one is polite?" (Expected answer: "Can I have a pencil, please?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says "Sorry, I can't." CCQ: "Is 'Sorry, I can't' a kind answer when you don't have something?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it's polite.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue showing two friends: Friend A needs an eraser, Friend B has none. Friend A asks politely, "Can I borrow an eraser, please?" Friend B responds, "Sorry, I can't help you. I don't have an eraser." The dialogue also includes a third friend offering, "I have one! Can I help you?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Making polite requests using "Can I have...please?" and expressing inability politely using "I can't..." or "Sorry, I can't help you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Teacher asks: "What does the first friend need?" (Expected answer: "An eraser.") "Does the second friend have an eraser?" (Expected answer: "No, he can't help.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Teacher reads statements based on the dialogue. Students show "True" or "False" with hand gestures. E.g., "The first friend asks rudely." (False). "The third friend is helpful." (True).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Rephrasing polite requests and responses for simplification using core vocabulary (pencil, paper, help, friend, please, thank you) and grammar (Can I have...please? I can't, I have/don't have). Focus on pragmatic use of polite expressions and sociolinguistic appropriate tone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Simplify the Request:</b> The teacher gives longer, informal requests. Students simplify them into polite, basic requests for a friend who is new to English. E.g., Teacher says: "Hey! Pass me that paper, quick!" Students mediate by saying: "Paper, please." or "Can I have paper, please?" (Teacher writes example on board and guides students.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Act it Out: Explaining a Polite Refusal:</b> The teacher shows flashcards (e.g., one hand with many pencils, another with only one).
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice asking: "Can I borrow a pencil, please?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When shown the "one pencil" card, students practice mediating the response: "Sorry, I can't help. Only one." or "I can't give you a pencil, I only have one." using gestures to show having only one item.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer-to-Peer Mediation Relay:</b> In pairs, Student A says a polite request (e.g., "Can I have a turn, please?"). Student B, acting as a mediator, rephrases it using simpler words and gestures for an imaginary "absent friend." (e.g., pointing to the game and saying "My turn, please?" or miming 'no turn'). Student A then gives a polite refusal or affirmation, and Student B mediates that response too.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will act as mediators to explain the "Project 2: Request Role-Play" instructions to a "friend who was absent." They will simplify the role-play steps and demonstrate key phrases using simple language and actions, focusing on making polite requests and responding politely when unable to help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired demonstration and simplified explanation of the "Request Role-Play."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired. One student is the "mediator" and the other is the "absent friend."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediator explains the "Request Role-Play" project. They simplify the instructions into 3-4 simple steps (e.g., "Step 1: Ask for something polite. Like 'pencil, please!'"; "Step 2: Say 'Sorry, I can't!' if you don't have it.") using basic vocabulary, gestures, and miming.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediator then briefly demonstrates a simple interaction from the role-play with the "absent friend," such as "Can I have your paper, please?" and "Sorry, I can't. I only have one." They use the learned polite request phrases and express inability politely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs then switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This task directly implements the theme "Sorry, I Can't Help You." and scenario "A Polite Request" by having students mediate and demonstrate the project that focuses on these communication acts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills, clarity of explanations, and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, individually or in pairs, will simplify and explain one part of the "Request Role-Play" instructions to the teacher (acting as a "new student") for 30 seconds. They must use simple words, target vocabulary, and at least one gesture. They will then demonstrate one polite request or polite refusal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Explains instructions simply and clearly (e.g., "Ask for a pencil. Say 'please'.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates words like "pencil," "paper," "help," "friend," "please," "thank you," "can't."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Uses "Can I have...please?" and "I can't..." or "Sorry, I can't help you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective Use of Gestures:</b> Uses gestures to aid understanding for simplifying concepts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Politeness:</b> Maintains a polite and friendly tone during the explanation and demonstration.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can explain to a friend how to ask for a _______ politely." (Students fill in the blank, e.g., 'pencil', 'paper').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is polite to say 'Sorry, I ______ help you' when I don't have something." (Students fill in the blank, e.g., 'can't').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="color: #1a5276; font-weight: bold; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain simply to my friend today about asking for things politely?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to explain using only simple words or gestures?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully simplify instructions and relay explanations using basic words and gestures? Was the target vocabulary used effectively in mediation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially for students struggling to simplify language at A1.1? (e.g., more visual cues, simpler core sentences to mediate).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did the Project 2: Request Role-Play connect to the mediation skill? Were students able to explain and demonstrate the role-play steps clearly?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson completes the "Sorry, I Can't Help You." theme and its "Request Role-Play" project. The mediation skills practiced here will be crucial for future collaborative projects and for supporting peers in understanding new concepts across different themes, reinforcing the importance of clear and simple communication in the classroom and beyond.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>